2 Requirements
Item Requirement
MATLAB R2025b or later (the script uses fig.Theme, available only in recent
releases).
1
Item Requirement
Toolboxes Statistics and Machine Learning Toolbox (mad, prctile); Image Processing
Toolbox recommended (imread, rgb2ind, ind2rgb, warp).
Memory The full cube is held in RAM (rows × columns × frames, double precision).
For 198 × 198 pixels and about 3,900 frames (two days at 45 s cadence) this
is about 1.2 GB per cube; the script keeps several cubes at once, so 16 GB
RAM or more is recommended.
OS Tested on Windows (default paths use D:\ ); Linux/macOS work after
changing the paths.
3 Step-by-step instructions
Step 1. Download the data
Download HMI_DATA.zip from Zenodo (<DOI>) and HMI_polarity_visualization_sunspot_20250917.m
from GitHub (or from the same Zenodo record).
Step 2. Unzip and place the data
Unzip HMI_DATA.zip so that the FITS files and ds9.tiff are located at:
D:\HMI_DATA\HMI_Fits\*.fits
D:\HMI_DATA\ds9.tiff
If you prefer another location (any drive, Linux/macOS), do not move files blindly: edit the two
path lines at the top of the script instead:
dataDir = ’D:\HMI_DATA\HMI_Fits\’; % folder containing the FITS files
P = imread(’D:\HMI_DATA\ds9.tiff’); % context image
Step 3. Open and run the code
1. Start MATLAB R2025b or newer.
2. Open HMI_polarity_visualization_sunspot_20250917.m.
3. Check the paths (Step 2) and the time-axis labels (Section 5).
4. Press Run. The two-day dataset (2025-09-17 to 2025-09-19) is processed in its entirety.
The script produces a full-screen 3D figure and saves it as Final_1.fig in the current MATLAB
folder.
Step 4. (Optional) Longer time intervals
The archive contains the two-day interval only. For a longer interval:
1. Open the header file supplied in the archive. It records the JSOC data series, the target
region/cutout, tracking, cadence, and time range used.
2. Go to the JSOC data export page: http://jsoc.stanford.edu/ajax/exportdata.html.
2
3. Reproduce the same query (same series, same cutout/tracking parameters and image size) but
with the desired start time and duration, then download the FITS files.
4. Place the FITS files in D:\HMI _DATA\HMI _Fits\ (or the folder set in dataDir).
5. Update the z-axis tick positions and labels (Section 5), since the number of frames
changes.
Important: all frames must have identical dimensions and the same cutout/tracking so that pixels
correspond between frames.
Step 5. Reproducing the 2017-05-19 dataset
The same procedure applies to the 2017-05-19 data (its header file is included in the archive):
1. Place that FITS set and its ds9.tiff at the locations in Step 2 (or change dataDir and the
imread path to point to the 2017 folders).
2. Update the z-axis tick positions and labels to the 2017-05-19 time range (Section 5).
3. Run the script as in Step 3. For longer intervals, follow Step 4 using the 2017 header file.
4 Method summary
1. Load all FITS magnetograms (cropped to the requested region).
2. Temporal equalization: each frame is re-centered and re-scaled using robust statistics (me-
dian and median absolute deviation) relative to a time-series-wide reference, removing frame-
to-frame photometric drift before thresholding.
3. Global percentile clipping: low/high percentiles are computed once over the full equalized
cube.
4. Polarity separation and thresholding: positive and negative pixels are separated by sign,
each normalized independently to [1, 100], and thresholded at (mean of the nonzero pixels)/0.8.
The two masks are checked to be mutually exclusive.
5. Volume rendering: the binary masks are scaled, smoothed with a 3D Gaussian kernel, and
displayed as isosurfaces with three-point lighting.
5 User-adjustable parameters
All parameters are at the top of the script unless stated otherwise.
Input and cropping
Parameter Default Description
dataDir ’D:\HMI
_DATA\HMI
_Fits\’
Folder containing the FITS magnetograms.
filePattern ’*.fits’ File-name pattern passed to dir. Files are
processed in the sorted order returned by dir, so
file names must sort chronologically.
3
Parameter Default Description
cropRowsRequested,
cropColsRequested
1:198 Pixel region (rows/columns) to analyze. It is
automatically clamped to the actual image size,
with a warning.
startIdx 1 Index of the first file to use. Increase it to skip
bad or incomplete initial frames.
P = imread(...) ds9.tiff Context image used as the base plane; flipud
aligns its orientation with the data.
Intensity normalization and thresholding
Parameter Default Description
lowPct, highPct 0.01, 99.9 Global percentiles used as the negative/positive
normalization limits. They control how strongly
extreme-field pixels influence the scaling.
Divisor 0.8 (in
threshold, thresholdR)
0.8 Polarity threshold = mean of nonzero normalized
pixels / 0.8. Increasing the divisor lowers the
threshold (more pixels included); decreasing it
raises the threshold (only stronger fields).
Volume smoothing and isosurface
Parameter Default Description
maskScale 35 Value assigned to masked voxels in the binary
volume; sets the amplitude of the volume
relative to isoLevel.
isoLevel 29 Isosurface level of the smoothed volume. Higher
values give tighter, smaller surfaces; lower values
give larger, more connected structures. Keep it
below maskScale.
smoothSigma 29 Size of the Gaussian kernel passed to smooth3
(third argument; the Gaussian sigma keeps the
MATLAB default). Larger values smooth more
strongly in space and time.
Base plane (context image)
Parameter Description
Zk2 =
20-((Xk-99).^2+(Yk-99).^2)./1500
Height of the base surface. The constant 20 sets its
vertical position; 1500 sets its curvature (larger =
flatter). The value 99 is the center of the 198 × 198 grid
and must be changed if the crop size changes.
meshgrid(1:198,1:198) Grid size of the base plane; must match the crop size.
set(h1,’FaceAlpha’,0.3) Transparency of the base image.
4
Appearance and view
Parameter Default Description
Face colors in
renderPolarityVolume(...)
red [0.86 0.08
0.24] (positive), blue
[0.25 0.41 0.88]
(negative)
RGB colors of the two polarities.
daspect(gca,[1 1 10]) [1 1 10] Aspect ratio; the last number stretches or
compresses the time axis.
ax.View [420, 17] Camera azimuth and elevation.
alpha(0.85) 0.85 Surface transparency.
applyStandardLighting() key/fill/rim Three-point lighting and material dull.
Light positions and colors can be edited in
the function.
colormap(hot(500)) hot Colormap applied to the base image.
Time-axis labels (must be edited for each dataset)
zticks([10, 957, 1914, ...]);
zticklabels({’17-Sep 14:00’, ’18-Sep 02:00’, ...});
Tick positions are frame indices and the labels are the corresponding UT times. In the supplied
script, 957 frames correspond to 12 hours, which implies the 45 s HMI cadence. For a different time
range, cadence, or number of frames, recompute the positions as frameIndex = (t − t0)/cadence, and
make sure the last tick does not exceed the number of frames in the cube.
Output
Item Description
savefig(gcf,’Final_1.fig’,’-v7.3’)Name and format of the saved figure.
Rotation movie block (commented) Uncomment to export an MP4 of the rotating 3D scene;
v.FrameRate and the azimuth step (0:2:360) control
speed and smoothness.
6 Data description and provenance
• Instrument/data: Solar Dynamics Observatory (SDO) / Helioseismic and Magnetic Imager
(HMI) line-of-sight magnetograms.
• Source: Joint Science Operations Center (JSOC), Stanford University, http://jsoc.stanford.
edu.
• Intervals provided: 2025-09-17 to 2025-09-19 and 2017-05-19.
• Format: FITS, one file per time step; image data read from the primary ’Image’ extension.
• Query details: data series, region/cutout, tracking, and cadence are documented in the
header files included in the archive.
5
7 Troubleshooting
Problem Likely cause / fix
Not enough FITS files
found
dataDir is wrong, or filePattern does not match the file
names.
Warning about clamped crop
region
The FITS frames are smaller than 198 × 198; adjust the crop
and the base-plane grid (meshgrid, center value 99).
Out of memory Reduce the crop region, use a shorter interval, or use a
machine with more RAM.
Figure appears empty or
sparse
Check that isoLevel is below maskScale and that the
thresholds and smoothSigma are not too restrictive.
Time labels do not match the
data
Recompute the z-tick positions (Section 5).
fig.Theme error Use MATLAB R2025b or newer, or remove that line.
